    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions: Reflective Systems and First-Return Structure\n\n## 1. Partial quotation for universal machines\n\n**Conjecture.** Every acceptable programming system admits a computable quotation map and a partial evaluator whose composite is the identity on all program states, while no such evaluator can decide every extensional predicate of those states.\n\nThe key insight is that self-simulation should be expressed as a retraction on a syntactically delimited domain, not as unrestricted access to semantic truth. **Why now?** The split between iterated retraction and Cantor obstruction gives exact positive and negative targets for a single computability-theoretic construction.\n\n## 2. Aperiodic depth versus recurrent semantic levels\n\n**Conjecture.** There exist finitely generated reflective transition systems with unbounded certified inspection depth but no periodic state, and systems of every finite first-return period whose certified inspection depth is exactly one.\n\nThe key insight is that reflective depth and dynamical period are independent invariants unless additional compatibility axioms connect them. **Why now?** The exact three-cycle and the unbounded iteration theorem isolate the two axes well enough to test their independence by explicit constructions.\n\n## 3. Heterogeneous three-level reflection\n\n**Conjecture.** For three state spaces connected by encoding and inspection maps, pairwise local retraction laws plus a coherent return law are sufficient to transport every invariant predicate around the entire cycle; dropping any one coherence law admits a finite counterexample.\n\nThe key insight is that a strange loop should be modeled as a cycle of split morphisms between different semantic levels rather than repeated endomorphisms of one undifferentiated space. **Why now?** The homogeneous three-state model supplies the base case, while heterogeneous coherence is the next test of whether the observed first-return phenomenon survives semantic typing.\n\n## 4. Quantitative robustness of approximate self-models\n\n**Conjecture.** In a metric state space, if inspection after encoding has error at most \u03b5 and both maps are Lipschitz, then the error after n reflective levels has a sharp geometric bound; below the contraction threshold the bound remains uniform in n.\n\nThe key insight is that exact retraction can be relaxed to controlled reconstruction error, turning reflective depth into a quantitative stability invariant. **Why now?** Exact iteration identifies the algebraic recurrence whose perturbation should produce the proposed geometric estimate.\n\n## 5. Fixed observations under restricted representability\n\n**Conjecture.** A representation map that is surjective only onto a transformation-closed family of observations forces a fixed observation for every endomorphism preserving that family, and this condition is optimal.\n\nThe key insight is that Lawvere diagonalization needs only enough representability to contain the diagonal observation generated by the chosen transformation. **Why now?** Total point-surjectivity is known to be too strong for predicate-valued observations, so a sharp restricted theorem could locate the exact boundary between fixed points and diagonal impossibility.\n",
