    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 The Spine of the Supersingular 2-Isogeny Graph\n\nThe results in `SupersingularSpinePath.lean` pin down the distance, eccentricity, diameter\nand mean-diameter functions of the path components of the spine for `\u2113 = 2` and\n`p \u2261 71, 119 (mod 120)`. The following conjectures extend that cycle.\n\n## 1. Mean diameter is an affine function of the component count\n\n**Conjecture.** For a spine with a fixed number `N` of `\ud835\udd3d_p`-rational supersingular\nvertices distributed among `k` path components, the mean diameter equals `N/k \u2212 1` exactly,\nand hence the multiset of mean diameters realizable at level `p` is determined by the\ndivisor-like fragmentation profile of `N` alone.\n\n*The key insight is* that the diameter of a path is one less than its vertex count, so\naveraging diameters only ever sees the total size and the number of components \u2014 every finer\nstructural detail cancels. *Why now?* The closed form `mean = total/k \u2212 1` is now proved for\nthe model, so the remaining step is to show the isogeny-theoretic spine components really are\npaths of the predicted sizes, turning a heuristic average into an exact arithmetic invariant.\n\n## 2. A central-limit law for spine mean diameters as `p` varies\n\n**Conjecture.** As `p` ranges over primes `\u2261 71, 119 (mod 120)` up to `X`, the normalized\nmean diameter of the spine, rescaled by its expected `\u0398(p)` growth, converges in\ndistribution to a fixed law governed by the statistics of `2`-part class-group ranks.\n\n*The key insight is* that the number of spine components is controlled by `2`-torsion in\nclass groups of imaginary quadratic orders, so the fluctuations of the mean diameter inherit\nthe (conjecturally Cohen\u2013Lenstra) fluctuations of those ranks. *Why now?* With the mean\ndiameter reduced to `total/k \u2212 1`, its distribution is entirely a question about the\ndistribution of the component count `k`, a quantity accessible to both explicit computation\nand known heuristics.\n\n## 3. Radius\u2013diameter rigidity of spine components\n\n**Conjecture.** Every spine component `P_m` satisfies `diameter = 2\u00b7radius` or\n`diameter = 2\u00b7radius \u2212 1`, and the parity is dictated by the residue of `m` modulo `2`; no\nspine component can be self-centered (radius equal to diameter) once it has more than two\nvertices.\n\n*The key insight is* that on a path the eccentricity profile is the symmetric \"V\"\n`max(i, n \u2212 i)`, forcing the radius to sit at the midpoint and the diameter at the ends, a\nrigidity absent from thicker graphs. *Why now?* The eccentricity function is now known in\nclosed form, so the radius is an explicit floor/ceiling expression and the rigidity is a\nfinite arithmetic check away from a theorem.\n\n## 4. Wiener index as a secondary discriminator\n\n**Conjecture.** When two spine structures share the same mean diameter, their total Wiener\nindices (summed over components) still differ, so the pair (mean diameter, total Wiener\nindex) separates strictly more spine structures than the mean diameter alone.\n\n*The key insight is* that the Wiener index of a path is cubic, `W(m) = (m\u00b3 \u2212 m)/3`, whereas\nthe diameter is linear, so the Wiener index is sensitive to a size imbalance between\ncomponents that the mean diameter averages away. *Why now?* The cubic closed form is proved\n(`wiener_interval`), making the two-invariant separation an explicit polynomial comparison.\n\n## 5. Stability of the metric functions under the deleted non-`\ud835\udd3d_p` edge\n\n**Conjecture.** Restoring the single non-`\ud835\udd3d_p` edge that was removed changes the diameter of\nthe affected component by at most one and never increases any eccentricity by more than one;\nconsequently the mean diameter of the full spine and of the edge-deleted spine differ by\n`O(1/k)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that a single extra edge can only create shortcuts of bounded benefit\nin an otherwise linear graph, so the metric functions are Lipschitz in that one structural\nperturbation. *Why now?* The edge-deleted metric is completely determined here, providing the\nexact baseline against which the one-edge perturbation can be measured.\n",
