    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## 1. Closed-subgroup exclusion for the Fibonacci four-anyon sector\n\nFix a published unitary normalization and fusion basis for the three-dimensional Fibonacci sector, and prove that every closed subgroup of \\(SU(3)\\) containing the three braid-generator matrices is \\(SU(3)\\) itself. This is falsifiable by exhibiting a proper closed subgroup containing all three matrices. **The key insight is** that universality is exactly a closed-subgroup classification problem, rather than a consequence of infinitude alone. **Why now?** The algebraic and topological certificates have isolated this as the decisive missing obligation and removed normalization-independent distractions.\n\n## 2. Exact infinite-order certificate for the three-generator product\n\nFor a fixed exact cyclotomic realization of the Fibonacci braid matrices, determine the characteristic polynomial of \\(\\rho(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\sigma_3)\\) and prove that at least one eigenvalue is not a root of unity. This is falsifiable by an exact finite-order computation. **The key insight is** that a non-torsion eigenvalue makes the full integer-power orbit injective and therefore certifies an infinite image. **Why now?** The abstract infinitude theorem identifies precisely the matrix-level fact that must be supplied, while avoiding unreliable floating-point order tests.\n\n## 3. Sector-correct dimension and determinant normalization\n\nDerive the Fibonacci fusion-space dimension from admissible fusion paths and identify the exact scalar correction that places the braid representation in \\(SU(3)\\) rather than merely \\(U(3)\\) or its projective quotient. This is falsifiable if the admissible-path count or corrected determinants disagree with three-dimensional special-unitary matrices. **The key insight is** that the supplied linear dimension formula describes neither the claimed three-dimensional sector nor its projective normalization. **Why now?** The parameter audit exposes a concrete inconsistency that must be repaired before any universality computation is interpretable.\n\n## 4. Quantitative compilation after qualitative density\n\nAssuming the closed-subgroup exclusion theorem, construct an inverse-closed finite braid gate set and derive explicit approximation-length bounds for metric balls in \\(SU(3)\\). Compare these bounds with direct braid searches at short word lengths. **The key insight is** that density supplies existence in every open target region, while efficient compilation requires a separate quantitative argument. **Why now?** The open-set hitting theorem provides the qualitative endpoint from which a rigorous Solovay--Kitaev analysis should begin.\n",
