    "description": "# Future Directions: Transform Uncertainty Beyond Fourier Duality\n\n## 1. Quantitative analytic-continuation uncertainty\n\n**Conjecture.** Let a normalized integral transform send compactly supported inputs to holomorphic functions on a connected complex domain, with a uniform growth bound determined by the input support. There is a quantitative lower bound on the transform's mass outside every measurable set of prescribed finite measure, depending only on the growth bound and the set's measure.\n\nThe key insight is that the qualitative identity principle proved in this cycle should admit a stable replacement through propagation-of-smallness estimates. **Why now?** The qualitative support obstruction and its exact regularity boundary have been isolated, leaving growth control as the single missing quantitative ingredient.\n\n## 2. Mellin uncertainty for multiplicatively porous sets\n\n**Conjecture.** If a function on the positive real axis and its Mellin transform are each concentrated on uniformly porous sets in logarithmic coordinates, their joint concentration decays exponentially with the number of resolved scales.\n\nThe key insight is that the Mellin transform becomes a Fourier transform after the logarithmic change of variables, while geometric progression becomes translation across logarithmic scales. **Why now?** The strip identity principle establishes qualitative rigidity, and multiscale porosity supplies a falsifiable route to a quantitative exponent.\n\n## 3. Radon strip-support stability\n\n**Conjecture.** For a compactly supported planar function, concentration in a strip and concentration of its Radon data in a finite-measure subset of angle-offset space obey a sharp lower bound controlled by strip width and angular coverage.\n\nThe key insight is that Radon uncertainty is governed by the Fourier-slice theorem and geometric range conditions rather than one-variable holomorphy. **Why now?** The adversarial review separated this case from Laplace and Mellin transforms, identifying the precise additional structure a valid theorem must exploit.\n\n## 4. Sparse learned-transform uniqueness\n\n**Conjecture.** An injective learned integral transform whose outputs satisfy a uniform analytic continuation and growth law admits a deterministic uniqueness certificate: no nonzero signal and transform code can both be supported on finite-measure sets.\n\nThe key insight is that sparse representation uniqueness can be derived from analytic rigidity rather than incoherence alone. **Why now?** The completed support theorem and the Hilbert-space Gram bound provide complementary qualitative and quantitative modules for such a certificate.\n\n## 5. A Kakeya-facing fractal uncertainty bridge\n\n**Conjecture.** A scale-uniform fractal uncertainty exponent for Fourier restrictions to families of Radon slices implies a nontrivial incidence bound for product-structured tube families.\n\nThe key insight is that simultaneous localization in position and slice frequency constrains how many directions can share the same spatial concentration. **Why now?** The qualitative continuation mechanism clarifies what must be strengthened, while the Radon exception identifies incidence geometry as the necessary replacement for holomorphic rigidity.\n",
