    "description": "# Future Directions: Signed Lattice Models for Knot Polynomials\n\n## 1. Diagram-local signed path model\n\n**Conjecture.** Every oriented knot diagram with `n` crossings admits a canonically defined forbidden region and a local sign attached to each surviving path from `(0,0)` to `(n,n)` such that the resulting signed area generating function is its normalized Alexander polynomial. The construction should intertwine Reidemeister moves with explicit sign-preserving, weight-preserving cancellations or bijections.\n\nThe key insight is that positivity is the only universal obstruction detected by coefficient counting; the Alexander state sum already supplies exactly the missing cancellation data.\n\n**Why now?** The unsigned formulation is ruled out even under arbitrary path deletion, while finite signed state sums are universal. The remaining test is therefore structural rather than existential: signs and paths must be extracted locally and naturally from a diagram.\n\n## 2. Alternating knots and unsigned absolute coefficients\n\n**Conjecture.** For every reduced alternating knot diagram, the coefficientwise absolute value of its normalized Alexander polynomial is the area generating function of paths avoiding a diagrammatically defined Ferrers-type region, after an explicit grading shift.\n\nThe key insight is that alternating Alexander polynomials have a controlled sign pattern, suggesting that cancellation can be factored into a global alternating character while the remaining magnitudes are honest counts.\n\n**Why now?** The torus family shows precisely how an alternating sign pattern obstructs unsigned counting while leaving positive magnitudes. A geometric model for those magnitudes would recover a substantial and sharply delimited part of the original proposal.\n\n## 3. Reciprocity from path reflection\n\n**Conjecture.** In every diagram-local signed path model, Alexander reciprocity is induced by an involution on admissible paths that preserves sign and sends area `a` to `c-a` for a diagram-dependent constant `c`.\n\nThe key insight is that palindromicity of a signed generating function follows from an area-reversing, sign-preserving involution; this converts a polynomial identity into a concrete combinatorial symmetry.\n\n**Why now?** The abstract involution criterion is established, so the conjecture has a precise falsifiable target: construct the involution from crossing data and check compatibility with the forbidden region.\n\n## 4. Connected sum as concatenation of path ensembles\n\n**Conjecture.** A canonical signed path construction can be chosen so that connected sum of knot diagrams corresponds to concatenation or Cartesian product of their path ensembles, with additive area and multiplicative sign.\n\nThe key insight is that product state spaces produce Cauchy products of signed generating functions, exactly matching multiplicativity of the Alexander polynomial under connected sum.\n\n**Why now?** The algebraic product law already holds for arbitrary signed state families. What remains is to enforce geometric locality strongly enough that the product operation agrees with an explicit gluing of knot diagrams.\n\n## 5. Higher-rank path models for HOMFLY-type state sums\n\n**Conjecture.** The two-variable HOMFLY polynomial admits a signed generating-function model using paths in a higher-dimensional lattice, with separate geometric statistics recording the two gradings and local forbidden structures determined by crossings.\n\nThe key insight is that the signed-state mechanism is independent of the number of gradings; replacing scalar area by a vector of lattice statistics should encode multivariable state-sum weights.\n\n**Why now?** The one-variable analysis cleanly separates the unavoidable sign data from the grading statistic. This supplies a controlled template for testing whether richer knot polynomials require genuinely new geometry or only additional lattice coordinates.\n",
