    "description": "# Future directions: the B\u00e1ez\u2013Duarte transform\n\n## 1. Quantitative passage from finite cutoffs to the full criterion\n\n**Conjecture.** For each fixed nonnegative integer `k`, the cutoff geometric\nmixtures with M\u00f6bius weights converge to the full B\u00e1ez\u2013Duarte coefficient, with\na truncation bound uniform for `k` in every interval `0 \u2264 k \u2264 N^\u03b1`, where some\nabsolute `\u03b1 > 0` is admissible.\n\nThe key insight is that the finite identity cleanly separates exact binomial\nalgebra from the tail of a reciprocal-square Dirichlet series. **Why now?** The\ncutoff formula exposes the tail term explicitly, so uniform estimates can be\nattacked without repeatedly rearranging infinite sums.\n\n## 2. Complete finite-difference hierarchy for positive measures\n\n**Conjecture.** If nonnegative weights `a_n` satisfy `\u2211 a_n/n\u00b2 < \u221e`, then the\nassociated infinite geometric mixture is completely monotone:\n`(-1)^r \u0394^r C_k \u2265 0` for all `r,k`, and every completely monotone sequence whose\nrepresenting measure is supported on `{1-n\u207b\u00b2 : n \u2265 1}` arises uniquely this way.\n\nThe key insight is that each forward difference multiplies a geometric mode by\n`-n\u207b\u00b2`, turning all orders of finite differences into reciprocal moments.\n**Why now?** The first-difference identity and positivity result identify the\ncorrect representation theorem and its exact support.\n\n## 3. Weighted Banach-space involution\n\n**Conjecture.** There is a sharp polynomial weight exponent for which the\nalternating binomial transform extends to a bounded involution on the resulting\nweighted sequence space, and this exponent matches the decay threshold in the\nB\u00e1ez\u2013Duarte criterion.\n\nThe key insight is that finite binomial inversion is exact, but infinite\ninversion is controlled by the interaction between Pascal growth and sequence\ndecay. **Why now?** The finite involution isolates the sole unresolved issue as\ncontinuity under completion, making a functional-analytic formulation precise.\n\n## 4. Divisor-incidence and binomial-incidence compatibility\n\n**Conjecture.** The divisor-lattice M\u00f6bius inversion and the Boolean-lattice\nbinomial inversion combine into a natural two-parameter convolution whose\nspectral decomposition simultaneously encodes reciprocal-power moments and\narithmetic divisor sums.\n\nThe key insight is that both inversion laws recover primitive data from\ncumulative data, but on different incidence algebras. **Why now?** Their verified\ncoexistence in the cutoff theory suggests a concrete tensor-product incidence\nalgebra rather than a merely metaphorical analogy.\n\n## 5. Rigidity of geometric supports\n\n**Conjecture.** A signed sequence with sufficiently rapid decay that admits two\nabsolutely summable geometric-mixture representations supported on\n`{1-n\u207b\u00b2 : n \u2265 1}` has identical weights term by term.\n\nThe key insight is that binomial inversion recovers every reciprocal moment, so\nuniqueness should follow from a discrete moment problem with a single\naccumulation point. **Why now?** The exact moment/coefficient equivalence supplies\nall moments and identifies the topology in which a uniqueness theorem is needed.\n",
